 \section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}
 % ============================================================
 
-\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} advancement of the Internet of Vehicles (IoV) and connected vehicles has established road-side infrastructure as an active participant in content distribution.
-In Content-Centric Vehicular Networks, Road-Side Units (RSUs) act as edge caching nodes to deliver requested content via Vehicle-to-Infrastructure (V2I) links \cite{1,2,3}.
-Because a vehicle typically traverses an RSU communication zone with highly dynamic stay times—often averaging around 100 seconds but escalating up to 300 seconds under traffic congestion or red-light signal phases, content unavailable at the moment of a request cannot always be fully transmitted before the vehicle exits the coverage area.
-The V2I communication channel provides the fundamental medium through which content chunks are continuously delivered to moving vehicles.
-Therefore, proactive caching, which places content at the RSU before demand materializes, is required to maintain service continuity across consecutive intersections \cite{4,5,6}.
-Understanding these complex interaction variables is paramount because vehicle residency times dictate the prefetching window size at the edge. By integrating vehicular dynamics directly into the scheduling logic, road-side infrastructure can dynamically allocate limited cache storage without centralized coordination.
-
-The performance of proactive caching strategies relies on estimating the duration a vehicle remains within the RSU coverage zone.
-The dynamic nature of this dwell time, which ranges from 100 to 300 seconds, poses a challenge for resource allocation.
-Underestimating the dwell time causes the scheduler to assume insufficient transmission time, resulting in under-prefetching and subsequent communication disruptions as vehicles depart before receiving their requested content.
-Conversely, overestimating this duration leads to over-prefetching, which occupies local cache storage with unused data and wastes backhaul link capacity.
-This misallocation of resources prevents the scheduler from optimizing priorities among competing requests when V2I bandwidth is limited.
-Thus, predicting the dwell time in an event-driven setting is a necessary requirement to minimize network overhead and maintain service quality in CCVNs \cite{7,8}.
-
-However, existing approaches face several critical limitations regarding their applicability to CCVN operations.
-First, most vehicular mobility prediction methods \cite{9,10} rely on continuous time-series inputs from globally collected sensor data.
-These approaches are structurally misaligned with the event-driven, single-snapshot inference environment of an RSU.
-When a request arrives, the RSU possesses only the instantaneous state of the vehicle without historical sequences, making continuous tracking via V2I uplink transmissions inefficient.
-Adapting sequence-based architectures to this setting introduces buffering delays, consumes bandwidth that should be reserved for content delivery, and degrades overall prediction accuracy.
-Second, prior studies on proactive caching \cite{11,12,13} focus primarily on content popularity modeling, often failing to estimate the explicit scalar dwell time required to schedule individual transfers.
-Third, existing methods frequently overlook the practical constraints of edge hardware by implicitly assuming centralized aggregation and inference using powerful servers \cite{14,15}.
-In a realistic CCVN, predictive models must operate within the limited computational and memory budgets of local RSUs, without relying on continuous cloud connectivity.
-
-\IEEEpubidadjcol
-To address these challenges, we propose \textbf{H-ST-MBAN}, a deterministic dwell-time predictor designed for event-driven, RSU-local inference.
-H-ST-MBAN captures a single snapshot of the vehicle state at the moment of a request, processing it immediately without historical buffering.
-The inference relies solely on the data payload embedded within the initial content request packet and local RSU sensor readings.
-To enable continuous local adaptation, we introduce a Dual-Model fine-tuning architecture where a globally pre-trained base model is deployed to the RSU.
-The RSU then fine-tunes a shadow model using a local snapshot queue, alternating roles with the active inference model to adapt to intersection-specific traffic dynamics without service interruption.
-In this context, H-ST-MBAN adopts an explicit deterministic regression architecture to estimate the dwell time of vehicles.
-This design optimizes the computational overhead at edge nodes by avoiding complex probabilistic sampling operations during inference.
-Thus, the deterministic approach minimizes processing latency, allowing the system to respond immediately to request packets.
-The resulting streamlined framework remains highly compatible with the resource-constrained environment of roadside units.
-
-In this work, we propose H-ST-MBAN to address the limitations of existing dwell-time prediction models in Content-Centric Vehicular Networks.
-We introduce a multi-branch neural network optimized for snap-shot edge inference alongside a local adaptation framework.
-These architectural and operational enhancements are designed to minimize network overhead and processing latency.
-Accurate dwell-time prediction enables edge routers to optimize prefetching decisions, thereby preventing both cache under-provisioning and backhaul traffic waste.
-The performance of the system is validated through extensive microscopic traffic simulations.
-The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows.
-\begin{itemize}
-    \item \textbf{Feature Partitioning:} We partition the input feature space into three semantically distinct branches, namely the Kinematic, Traffic Control, and Social/Contextual branches, where each branch is encoded by a dedicated ResBlock to prevent gradient conflicts across heterogeneous variable groups.
-    \item \textbf{Branch-level Attention:} We introduce a multi-head self-attention mechanism over branch-level representations, allowing the model to aggregate branch contributions dynamically according to the current traffic context.
-    \item \textbf{Hybrid Dual-Stream Architecture:} We incorporate an XGBoost-based tabular prior stream combined with spatio-temporal neural network branches using a learnable gating fusion mechanism to capture decision boundaries.
-    \item \textbf{SUMO-based Feature Augmentation:} We augment the input feature set with twelve variables obtained from the SUMO microscopic traffic simulator, capturing fine-grained local dynamics including leader-follower gaps, lane occupancy, and intersection queue lengths.
-    \item \textbf{Steady-State Filtering:} We incorporate a steady-state filtering mechanism that stabilizes gradient updates, mitigating the degradation of model weight parameters caused by outlier variables during local adaptation.
-    \item \textbf{Local Ensemble Fine-Tuning:} We resolve the limitations of tree ensembles by enabling local fine-tuning of the tabular model, allowing it to track changing spatial-temporal vehicular dynamics at edge nodes.
-    \item \textbf{Ping-Pong Update Strategy:} We propose a ping-pong update strategy that fine-tunes a shadow model using a local snapshot queue while the primary model serves requests, ensuring service continuity without blocking real-time inference.
-\end{itemize}
+\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} development of the Internet of Vehicles (IoV) and autonomous driving has increased vehicular data generation. According to the Ericsson Mobility Report, global mobile network data traffic is projected to reach 515 Exabytes per month by 2031 \cite{36}. To deliver high-bandwidth content to moving vehicles without overloading the core network, proactive edge caching via RSUs in CCVNs is utilized as a foundational infrastructure \cite{2,3,4}. However, the Vehicle-to-Infrastructure (V2I) transmission window is constrained by the dynamic dwell time of the vehicle at the intersection, which fluctuates between 100 to 300 seconds due to traffic signal phases and queueing densities. If this residency duration is not accurately predicted, the RSU misallocates local resources, resulting in either over-prefetching that wastes edge storage and backhaul bandwidth, or under-prefetching that causes service interruptions when vehicles exit the coverage area \cite{5,6,7}. Therefore, real-time dwell-time prediction at the edge is a prerequisite for optimizing proactive caching schedules and ensuring service continuity in CCVNs.
+
+Designing a mobility prediction model for proactive edge caching requires balancing low-latency constraints with the need for adaptability to local traffic dynamics. To maximize predictive accuracy, recent sequence-based forecasting architectures and federated learning frameworks extract continuous temporal dependencies \cite{9,12}. However, the continuous uplink tracking and repetitive parameter exchanges required by these models increase V2I bandwidth usage and introduce buffering delays, which contradicts the event-driven nature of immediate edge caching \cite{10,11}. Conversely, gradient-boosted decision trees (GBDTs) can eliminate tracking latency by executing inferences on a single snapshot without historical buffering. Despite their communication efficiency, these tree ensembles are constrained by static global weights, limiting their capacity to adapt to the localized spatio-temporal conditions of individual intersections. Furthermore, mapping heterogeneous tabular data directly through standard deep neural networks results in gradient interference and degraded learning performance. Therefore, existing methodologies remain confined within a structural trade-off, where they either prioritize communication efficiency at the cost of adaptive local precision, or vice versa.
+
+To address these structural and algorithmic limitations, this paper proposes the Hybrid Spatio-Temporal Multi-Branch Attention Network, denoted as H-ST-MBAN, for proactive edge caching in CCVNs. The primary contribution of this paper involves the design of a specialized vehicular communication protocol and edge table management scheme that autonomously constructs local datasets by piggybacking state variables onto content request packets and logging targets via asynchronous exit events. Building upon this data foundation, we propose an event-driven regression framework that predicts intersection dwell time using a single vehicular snapshot extracted from the initial content request packet, which eliminates the need for continuous historical buffering. To process this snapshot data without gradient interference, we design a dual-stream architecture that combines a gradient boosting ensemble with a multi-branch neural network to isolate and learn the complex spatio-temporal dynamics of heterogeneous traffic variables. Furthermore, we introduce a decentralized updating strategy that fine-tunes the network using strictly local intersection data, allowing the model to adapt to localized traffic variations without incurring uplink parameter exchange overheads. Finally, we validate the proposed framework using microscopic traffic traces generated via the SUMO simulator to confirm that the localized architecture maintains target cache hit rates while satisfying the strict latency constraints of vehicular environments.
 
 The remainder of this paper is organized as follows.
 Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work on vehicular caching, mobility prediction, and dwell-time estimation.
