-\subsection{H-ST-MBAN Dwell Time Estimation}
-\label{sec.prop.A}
-% ============================================================
-
-\textbf{Definition 1 (RSU-Local Snapshot Inference).}
-At the moment vehicle $v$ issues a content request to its serving RSU $r$, a single feature vector $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{30}$ is assembled.
-This vector aggregates the real-time state of $v$ and its local traffic environment as observed exclusively by $r$.
-The predictor $f_r : \mathbb{R}^{30} \to \mathbb{R}^2$ subsequently maps this snapshot to two scalar dwell-time estimates represented by \hat{\tau}_\text{cur} and \hat{\tau}_\text{nxt}.
-This mapping executes in one forward pass, with no buffering of historical observations.
-Therefore, the model avoids unnecessary data exchange and communication with other RSUs during the inference stage. This decentralized design ensures that localized traffic anomalies do not propagate errors to adjacent intersection controllers, improving global network resilience.
-
-The overall H-ST-MBAN architecture consists of a dual-stream design: a GBDT Prior Stream and a Spatio-Temporal Neural Network Stream, combined via Learnable Gating Fusion, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:arch}.
-The 30 input features are directly fed into a fully-optimized XGBoost model to generate a base tabular prediction.
-In parallel, the identical features are partitioned into three semantically distinct subsets for the neural network stream: the kinematic branch represented by $\mathbf{x}^K \in \mathbb{R}^{13}$, the traffic control branch represented by $\mathbf{x}^T \in \mathbb{R}^{6}$, and the social/contextual branch represented by $\mathbf{x}^S \in \mathbb{R}^{11}$.
-Each subset is encoded independently by a dedicated branch encoder to a shared dimension $d_\text{branch}$, yielding branch representations $Z_k$, $Z_t$, and $Z_s$.
-These three representations are treated as a 3-token sequence and fused by a multi-head self-attention layer.
-The fused embedding is decoded by four consecutive ResBlocks, which forms a deep ResNet, to learn the spatio-temporal residual errors not captured by the GBDT stream.
-Finally, the joint prediction represented by \hat{\tau}_\text{cur} and \hat{\tau}_\text{nxt} is produced by dynamically gating the outputs of both streams, trained under a combined Huber loss function.
-
-\begin{figure}[!t]
-\centering
-\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{./figure/architecture.pdf}
-\caption{Overall architecture of H-ST-MBAN. A GBDT prior stream (XGBoost) provides a base prediction from tabular features. In parallel, three branch encoders process the Kinematic, Traffic Control, and Social/Contextual features. The T Branch applies Cyclical Temporal Encoding to signal phase variables. Branch representations $Z_k$, $Z_t$, $Z_s$ are treated as a 3-token sequence and fused by multi-head self-attention. The neural network stream focuses on learning residual errors through a deep four-layer ResNet. The outputs from both streams are combined via learnable gating to produce final joint predictions $\hat{\tau}_\text{cur}$ and $\hat{\tau}_\text{nxt}$ under Huber loss.}
-\label{fig:arch}
-\end{figure}
-
-The design rationale for the three-branch decomposition follows from the distinct inductive biases required by each variable group.
-Kinematic variables encode continuous physical quantities governed by Newtonian kinematics.
-Thus, a dedicated Residual Block with standard ReLU activations is well suited to learning their nonlinear interactions without vanishing gradients.
-Traffic control variables carry phase periodicity that a raw integer representation cannot convey, so Cyclical Temporal Encoding resolves phase discontinuities before any learnable layer is applied.
-Social and contextual variables encode queue and density signals whose relevance varies across intersections.
-Thus, a specialized weighting block assigns RSU-specific feature-wise coefficients, allowing each RSU instance to emphasize the social signals that are most predictive at its own intersection.
-Fusing the resulting branch tokens through MHA rather than simple concatenation permits the model to reweight branch contributions based on the current snapshot, without coupling the encoding gradients of different variable groups.
-Rather than forcing the deep neural network to learn sharp, discontinuous tabular boundaries from scratch, H-ST-MBAN leverages an ensemble stacking approach.
-The XGBoost prior resolves tabular splits, leaving the branch encoders and subsequent deep residual layers to model complex, non-linear spatio-temporal correlations.
-
-% ============================================================
-
-\subsubsection{Kinematic Encoder}
-
-The Kinematic branch encodes 13 continuous variables that characterize vehicle speed, inter-vehicle spacing, and geometric distances to RSU coverage boundaries. Specifically, these parameters consist of $r_\text{cov}$, $\text{dirct}$, $d_{l,c}$, $d_{e,n}$, $d_{l,n}$, $d_\text{rsu}$, $v_{c,a}$, $v_{n,a}$, $v_\text{ahead,avg}$, $d_\text{leader}$, $v_\text{leader}$, $\text{est\_travel\_time}$, and $\text{route\_lane\_changes}$. These variables are numerically heterogeneous in scale, so a learned linear projection precedes the nonlinear encoder. The K Branch encoder consists of a linear projection followed by a standard ReLU ResBlock: \begin{equation} Z_k = \text{ResBlock}_\text{K}\!\left(W_K\, \mathbf{x}^K + b_K\right), \quad Z_k \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{branch}} \label{eq:zk} \end{equation} where $W_K \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{branch} \times 13}$. The standard ReLU activation, coupled with Batch Normalization within the residual connection, provides gradient propagation for continuous inputs. The residual connection allows the block to refine rather than overwrite the initial projection. Equation~(\ref{eq:zk}) maps the 13-dimensional input to the shared branch dimension $d_\text{branch}$.
-
-\subsubsection{Traffic Control Encoder}
-
-The Traffic Control branch encodes 6 variables related to signal state and phase timing: $\text{tls}_c$, $\text{tls}_n$, $\text{tlt}_c$, $\text{tlt}_n$, $q_{\text{len},c}$, and $q_{\text{len},n}$. Signal phase variables are periodic; representing them as raw scalars introduces an artificial discontinuity at cycle boundaries that impedes gradient-based learning. CTE resolves this by mapping each phase scalar $t$ onto the unit circle: \begin{equation} \phi(t,\, T) = \left(\sin\!\left(\frac{2\pi\, t}{T}\right),\ \cos\!\left(\frac{2\pi\, t}{T}\right)\right) \label{eq:cte} \end{equation} where $T$ is the relevant period, with $T_\text{sig} = 90$\,s representing the signal state and $T_\text{phase} = 30$\,s representing the time-to-phase-change. CTE is applied independently to $\text{tls}_c$, $\text{tls}_n$, $\text{tlt}_c$, and $\text{tlt}_n$, expanding these 4 scalars to 8 values. The two queue-length variables $q_{\text{len},c}$, $q_{\text{len},n}$ are passed through unchanged, yielding a 10-dimensional encoded vector $\tilde{\mathbf{x}}^T \in \mathbb{R}^{10}$. The T Branch encoder then maps this vector through a linear projection and a ResBlock: \begin{equation} Z_t = \text{ResBlock}_\text{T}\!\left(W_T\, \tilde{\mathbf{x}}^T + b_T\right), \quad \tilde{\mathbf{x}}^T = \bigl[\phi(\text{tls}_c,T_\text{sig}),\, \phi(\text{tls}_n,T_\text{sig}),\, \phi(\text{tlt}_c,T_\text{phase}),\, \phi(\text{tlt}_n,T_\text{phase}),\, q_{\text{len},c},\, q_{\text{len},n}\bigr] \label{eq:zt} \end{equation} where $W_T \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{branch} \times 10}$ and $Z_t \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{branch}}$. Because the dominant nonlinearity is resolved analytically by CTE, the downstream ResBlock focuses on learning interactions among the encoded traffic variables.
-
-\subsubsection{Social/Contextual Encoder}
-
-The Social branch encodes 11 variables that describe surrounding vehicle density, queue occupancy, and merge-lane competition. These variables consist of $n_{t,0}$ through $n_{t,3}$, $n_\text{cur}$, $n_\text{nxt}$, $n_{\text{ahead},c}$, $n_{\text{ahead},n}$, $\text{occ}_c$, $\text{occ}_n$, and $n_\text{merge}$.
-The relevance of these variables depends on the geometry and signal timing of the specific RSU. For instance, the same queue length may be critical at a narrow single-lane intersection yet largely irrelevant at a wide multi-lane junction.
-A feature-wise attention mechanism based on the SE block~\cite{29} is applied before the residual encoder.
-This architectural choice enables each computational node to calibrate its own feature dependencies based on local sensor data.
-Thus, each RSU instance learns which density signals are most critical at its own intersection.
-Such localized adjustments are necessary because different intersections exhibit distinct bottleneck behaviors.
-The resulting attention-weighted tensor is then passed into the subsequent residual encoding stages:
+The originating RSU merges the received external data with its local PDT entries using the vehicle and content identifiers as relational keys.
+To maximize representational efficiency, the integrated data elements are categorized into discrete semantic branches prior to computation.
+The resulting feature vector and its corresponding domain categories are structurally defined as presented in Table~\ref{tab:features}.
+Because this formulation constructs an instantaneous snapshot utilizing small control packets during ongoing content provision, it reduces the overall communication load compared to continuous time-series trajectory tracking.
+The separation between background feature aggregation and active content delivery guarantees that physical service quality remains uncompromised.
+This domain-specific categorization empowers the computational framework to extract localized traffic characteristics without relying on a centralized cloud architecture.
+By independently resolving contextual metrics at the network edge, the system mitigates the bottleneck phenomena typically observed in centralized processing architectures.
+
+\subsection{Dwell Time Inference and Model Architecture}
+\label{sec.prop.B}
+
+To determine the precise volume of proactive chunk prefetching at the subsequent infrastructure node, the active Road-Side Unit (RSU) processes the aggregated feature vector from the Pending Data Table (PDT) to infer the current communication residency time ($t_{cur, dw}$) and the anticipated next residency time ($t_{nxt, dw}$).
+Specifically, the system addresses the extreme volatility of Vehicular-to-Infrastructure (V2I) environments by proposing the Hybrid Spatio-Temporal Multi-Branch Attention Network (H-ST-MBAN), which is meticulously designed to accommodate heterogeneous sensor modalities.
+During the initial deployment phase where a pre-trained neural network remains unavailable, the RSU defaults to a deterministic cold-start fallback mechanism that approximates the target durations algebraically.
+This analytical fallback computes the residency periods by dividing the remaining route distances, derived from the Wireless Access in Vehicular Environments (WAVE) standard boundaries, by the instantaneous spatial mean velocity of the surrounding traffic.
+Nevertheless, despite operating under this heuristic approximation, the communication framework continuously maintains the PDT and the internal dataset accumulation queues to ensure comprehensive data collection for subsequent optimization procedures.
+
+Once the pre-trained H-ST-MBAN model becomes fully disseminated across the network edges, the infrastructure nodes seamlessly transition to executing precise multi-variable neural inferences.
+Unlike homogeneous visual or sequential structures, vehicular tabular data inherently exhibits massive discrepancies in physical scaling properties, encompassing kinematic, spatial, and topological dynamics.
+Consequently, these structural variations frequently induce statistical outliers associated with sudden localized congestion phenomena or unanticipated vehicular collisions.
+Conventional recurrent architectures or simplistic multi-layer perceptrons conventionally concatenate these heterogeneous arrays, forcing the optimization space to be dominated by features exhibiting disproportionately massive magnitudes.
+Furthermore, this elementary concatenation inevitably triggers gradient vanishing or explosion during the continuous online fine-tuning processes at the computational edges, massively exacerbating the phenomenon of catastrophic forgetting.
+
+In order to fundamentally overcome these structural limitations and maximize operational reliability within the constrained V2I paradigm, the proposed H-ST-MBAN framework is constructed around four integral architectural methodologies.
+First, the architecture performs Heterogeneous Feature Extraction via Pre-activation Residual Blocks to strictly isolate the disparate scaling metrics by independently processing the respective categorical domains.
+Mathematically, for a given arbitrary input vector partition $\mathbf{x}_i$, the pre-activation non-linear transformation is defined as follows:
 \begin{equation}
-\mathbf{a} = \sigma\!\left(W_2 \cdot \text{ReLU}(W_1 \cdot \mathbf{x}^S + b_1) + b_2\right), \quad \mathbf{a} \in [0,1]^{11}
-\label{eq:se_attn}
+\mathbf{z}_i = \mathbf{x}_i + \mathcal{F}(\text{ReLU}(\text{BN}(\mathbf{x}_i)), \mathbf{W}_i)
 \end{equation}
+where $\text{BN}(\cdot)$ denotes batch normalization, $\mathcal{F}(\cdot)$ represents the residual mapping function, and $\mathbf{W}_i$ incorporates the localized learnable weights.
+This explicit pre-activation structural formulation aggressively normalizes the domain-specific tensors prior to any dense linear projection, subsequently circumventing the risk of numerical gradient explosion.
+Ultimately, this independent feature refinement isolates the kinematic, topological, and spatial domains into a uniform high-dimensional latent space without prematurely imposing deterministic feature fusion.
+
+Second, the system incorporates Domain-Level Multi-Head Attention Fusion by processing the independently encoded latent domain vectors as discrete and sequential embedded tokens.
+Instead of relying on rudimentary flat tensor concatenations, the proposed framework leverages the scaled dot-product attention paradigm to dynamically compute the complicated cross-domain interactions.
+The attention output for the query matrices ($\mathbf{Q}$), key matrices ($\mathbf{K}$), and value matrices ($\mathbf{V}$) is mathematically derived through the following expression:
 \begin{equation}
-Z_s = \text{ResBlock}_\text{S}\!\left(W_S\,(\mathbf{a} \odot \mathbf{x}^S) + b_S\right), \quad Z_s \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{branch}}
-\label{eq:zs}
+\text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V}) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{K}^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)\mathbf{V}
 \end{equation}
-where $\odot$ denotes element-wise multiplication, $W_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{4 \times 11}$ and $W_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{11 \times 4}$ are the SE reduction and expansion weights, which feature a reduction ratio of 4, and $W_S \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{branch} \times 11}$. Because each RSU trains its own model, the weights $W_1$ and $W_2$ encode the idiosyncratic traffic patterns of that intersection, making the Social branch the primary carrier of RSU-local knowledge. To integrate this localized contextual knowledge with the other two representations, the three branch representations $Z_k$, $Z_t$, and $Z_s \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{branch}}$ are combined into a single unified embedding. Rather than using naive concatenation followed by a fully connected layer, which would allow the fusion layer to freely mix features across branches and potentially reintroduce gradient interference between heterogeneous variable groups, H-ST-MBAN treats each branch representation as a single token and applies multi-head self-attention~\cite{30} over the resulting 3-token sequence. This explicit attention mechanism preserves the isolated gradient pathways established during the initial encoding phase, preventing the heterogeneous variables from disrupting each other during the continuous local fine-tuning cycles. Specifically, we define the concatenated branch tensor as $\mathbf{Z} = [Z_k;\, Z_t;\, Z_s] \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times d_\text{branch}}$, and for each attention head $i = 1, \ldots, h$, the model computes the self-attention projections as follows:
+where $d_k$ represents the defined scaling dimension of the embedded latent space.
+This sophisticated attention allocation dynamically evaluates the complex contextual interdependence connecting topological traffic signals with physical queue formations and real-time velocity arrays.
+Consequently, the final aggregated attention scores produce a highly contextualized fusion representation that accurately weights the localized infrastructural constraints critically affecting subsequent vehicular trajectories.
+
+Third, the predictive system ensures Robust Deterministic Decoding against Outliers by mapping the multi-head attention weighted tensors through a serialized set of residual decoders directly yielding deterministic time scalars.
+Because the underlying task necessitates precise temporal intervals for the content caching schedules, the decoding architecture aggressively discards probabilistic generative distributions to prioritize absolute point-estimate stability.
+To further stabilize the backward optimization gradients against sudden traffic anomalies, the model rigorously employs the continuous $L_1$ Mean Absolute Error metric defined as:
 \begin{equation}
-Q_i = \mathbf{Z}\, W^Q_i,\quad K_i = \mathbf{Z}\, W^K_i,\quad V_i = \mathbf{Z}\, W^V_i
-\label{eq:mha_proj}
-\end{equation}
-\begin{equation}
-\text{head}_i = \text{softmax}\!\left(\frac{Q_i K_i^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right) V_i,\quad d_k = \frac{d_\text{branch}}{h}
-\label{eq:mha_head}
+\mathcal{L}_{\text{MAE}} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{j=1}^{N} \left| y_j - \hat{y}_j \right|
 \end{equation}
+where $y_j$ and $\hat{y}_j$ denote the ground-truth and inferred temporal residency scalars across the processed vehicular batches.
+This intentional architectural departure from standard squared error objectives naturally bounds the magnitude of gradient updates directly provoked by severe statistical outliers inherent in complex vehicular networks.
+Thus, the network parameter optimization reliably converges toward globally optimal configurations, regardless of the instantaneous extreme congestion fluctuations frequently observed within the designated intersection coverage zones.
+
+Finally, the integrated framework implements an Adaptive Residual Prior Learning methodology designed to seamlessly hybridize non-linear neural processing with the structural robustness of gradient-boosted decision trees.
+The ultimate contextual dwell time inference explicitly fuses the dual predictive mechanisms through the formulated adaptive gating equation:
 \begin{equation}
-\mathbf{H} = \text{LayerNorm}\!\left(\text{Concat}(\text{head}_1,\ldots,\text{head}_h)\, W^O + \mathbf{Z}\right), \quad \mathbf{H} \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times d_\text{branch}}
-\label{eq:mha_out}
+\text{Pred}_{\text{final}} = \alpha \times \text{ML\_Pred} + \beta \times \text{NN\_Pred}
 \end{equation}
-where $W^Q_i, W^K_i, W^V_i \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{branch} \times d_k}$ and $W^O \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{branch} \times d_\text{branch}}$.
-The residual connection and LayerNorm in Equation~(\ref{eq:mha_out}) follow the standard pre-norm transformer formulation.
-The network is securely configured with $h = 4$ heads and $d_\text{branch} = 128$, yielding $d_k = 32$.
-By utilizing these multi-head projections, the model accurately calibrates the combined influence of kinematic, traffic, and social data.
-As a result, the network produces an intermediate state ready for the deeper decoding stages. The layer normalization step stabilizes the activation distributions, which prevents the subsequent deep residual decoder from suffering from covariate shift during continuous local updates.
-
-Because attention weights are explicitly conditioned on the current snapshot, the model dynamically adjusts branch contributions to reflect the dominant bottleneck factor in each scenario.
-The resulting fused tensor $\mathbf{H}$ is then systematically flattened to $\mathbb{R}^{3\,d_\text{branch}}$.
-Subsequently, it is projected back down to $\mathbb{R}^{d_\text{branch}}$ by a final linear layer accompanied by LayerNorm.
-This specific dimensional reduction strictly controls the capacity of the feature bottleneck before it enters the residual decoder.
-As a result, the deep residual layers can focus entirely on mapping the unified embedding to the physical output variables required by the CCVN precaching scheduler.
