-Rather than forcing the neural network to learn both sharp tabular splits and complex spatio-temporal interactions from scratch, H-ST-MBAN employs a residual prior learning approach via ensemble stacking. The GBDT Prior Stream processes the raw snapshot features using a fully-optimized XGBoost model. This tree-based ensemble captures the discontinuous, hard boundaries prevalent in vehicular tabular data. The integration of this prior stream reduces the burden on the deeper neural layers. The base prediction generated by this stream is denoted as $P_\text{XGB} = \text{XGBoost}(\mathbf{X}) \in \mathbb{R}^2$. The final prediction combines the tabular prior and the neural network residual using learnable scalar gates $\alpha, \beta \in \mathbb{R}^2$: \begin{equation} \hat{\mathbf{y}} = P_\text{XGB} \odot \alpha + P_\text{NN} \odot \beta \label{eq:fusion_gate} \end{equation} where $\alpha$ is initialized to $1.0$ and $\beta$ is initialized to $0.0$. This specific initialization mathematically guarantees that the H-ST-MBAN model begins training exactly at XGBoost's performance level. Over the course of training, gradient descent adjusts $\alpha$ and $\beta$ alongside the neural network parameters. This adaptive gating allows the deep learning branches to strictly improve upon the GBDT baseline by learning the spatio-temporal residual errors that XGBoost misses. Both dwell-time targets are regressed simultaneously under a shared loss, allowing the decoder to exploit correlated causal factors.
 
-% ============================================================
+The continuous aggregation of these verified ground truth pairs governs the localized model adaptation under a two-track operation protocol designed to ensure zero service interruption.
+Depending on the current architectural state, the training process executes in either a centralized pre-training phase or an autonomous online fine-tuning phase.
+During the initial deployment phase, the active RSUs lack pre-trained neural parameters and accumulate the empirical data records into their localized storage queues.
+Once a sufficient volume of data accumulates, the infrastructure transmits these dense batches to a centralized cloud server, which aggregates the regional observations to execute global pre-training.
+Following the mathematical convergence of the global model, the central server distributes the identical optimized parameter weights back to every participating RSU.
+Upon receiving these initialized parameters, the edge nodes sever their training dependency on the central server and transition into an autonomous, localized operational state.
+This localized architecture eliminates continuous uplink training bottlenecks while reacting to specific intersection anomalies and localized concept drift phenomena.
 
-Training minimizes the sum of per-output Huber losses:
-\begin{equation}
-\mathcal{L}_\delta(\mathbf{y}, \hat{\mathbf{y}}) = \sum_{i \in \{\text{cur},\,\text{nxt}\}} \ell_\delta(\tau_i,\, \hat{\tau}_i)
-\label{eq:total_loss}
-\end{equation}
-\begin{equation}
-\ell_\delta(y,\, \hat{y}) =
-\begin{cases}
-\dfrac{1}{2}(y - \hat{y})^2 & \text{if } |y - \hat{y}| \le \delta \\[6pt]
-\delta\,|y - \hat{y}| - \dfrac{1}{2}\delta^2 & \text{otherwise}
-\end{cases}
-\label{eq:huber}
-\end{equation}
-with $\delta = 1.0$ fixed throughout all experiments.
-The piecewise definition in Equation~(\ref{eq:huber}) provides MSE-like behavior in the normal operating range $|y - \hat{y}| \le \delta$, yielding smooth gradients during typical training steps, while growing linearly for residuals beyond $\delta$ so that outlier samples cannot dominate the gradient update.
-In the snapshot-based CCVN setting, dwell-time outliers arise naturally from vehicles caught by extended red phases or anomalous queue build-up. These samples would inflate MSE gradients and bias the model toward worst-case scenarios.
-Huber loss bounds their influence while preserving informative gradients for the majority of predictions.
-A fixed $\delta = 1.0$ second corresponds to the typical per-step dwell-time measurement resolution in the SUMO simulation and was confirmed on a held-out validation split.
+To execute this continuous local adaptation without introducing latency into the active caching decisions, each RSU natively operates two independent model instances following a distinct ping-pong logic.
+The content provisioning and dwell-time inference exclusively rely on the active inference model, which utilizes the most recently optimized neural weights to process incoming client requests.
+Simultaneously, the background standby model monitors the localized dataset queue and initiates the parameter fine-tuning process the moment the accumulated records exceed the defined capacity threshold.
+When the background model successfully finalizes its localized gradient descent updates, the RSU executes a role exchange, promoting the updated model to handle all subsequent inference operations.
+The outdated inference model is permitted to conclude its final ongoing predictive calculations before being structurally demoted to the standby role.
+Upon demotion, the system flushes the processed dataset queue, allowing the newly assigned standby model to begin accumulating a fresh batch of physical observations for the subsequent optimization cycle.
+This uninterrupted alternation mechanism guarantees that predictive precaching operates continuously within urban communication networks.
 
+\n\section{Performance Evaluations}\label{sec:experiments}
 % ============================================================
 
+\subsection{Experimental Setup}
+To validate the proposed H-ST-MBAN architecture, we construct a microscopic traffic environment utilizing the Simulation of Urban MObility (SUMO) framework. The CCVN environment is explicitly modeled around complex multi-lane intersection topologies, utilizing the Desk02 and Desk01 urban road network layouts to capture realistic vehicle trajectories. Within this simulation, vehicles are governed by advanced car-following and lane-changing models that replicate real-world human driving behaviors, including acceleration, deceleration, and lane-merging dynamics. The intersection traffic signals operate on fixed periodic cycles, with the total signal phase duration $T_\text{sig}$ set to 90 seconds and individual phase transitions $T_\text{phase}$ occurring every 30 seconds. To emulate a wide spectrum of congestion states, the simulations inject vehicles at varying generation rates, resulting in traffic densities that span continuously from sparse conditions at 5 veh/km/lane to heavily congested scenarios reaching 25 veh/km/lane.
 
+The networking layer of the simulation implements specific communication parameters to govern the V2I interactions. Each RSU is positioned at the geometric center of major intersections and provides a fixed omnidirectional communication coverage radius of 800 meters. Consecutive RSUs are deployed at an inter-RSU spacing of 2,400 meters, intentionally creating an 800-meter shadow region between adjacent coverage zones where no V2I signal is available. This topology forces the proactive caching scheduler to perfectly time its content delivery before the vehicle enters the dead zone. The V2I links are modeled with standard vehicular networking bandwidth constraints, where packet delivery success rates and channel signal-to-noise ratios fluctuate based on instantaneous vehicle density and physical distance to the RSU antenna. These strict communication parameters ensure that the predictive models are evaluated under realistic bandwidth constraints and edge-caching deadlines.
 
+To execute the computational aspects of the framework, we utilize a heterogeneous hardware environment that separates global pre-training from local fine-tuning. The initial global pre-training phase is conducted on a high-performance centralized workstation equipped with an Intel Core i9-10900X CPU, 128 GB of DDR4 RAM, and four NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPUs. This massive computational capacity allows the core server to rapidly process the aggregated global dataset and establish the initial prior weights for the neural network. Conversely, the local fine-tuning phase is simulated under the strict resource constraints typical of standard RSU edge hardware. The edge nodes operate with limited thermal and electrical budgets, restricting peak memory consumption to under 50 MB and requiring fast execution times to prevent inference bottlenecks. This dual-tier hardware configuration accurately reflects the practical deployment constraints of modern intelligent transportation systems.
 
